nnot separate real defensive adjustment from offense executing differently; near-uniform transition matrices consistent with label noise.\n- observed_sample_and_power: 523 cells; 440 pairs\n- auto_validation_status: no_validation_recorded; evidence: NOT_RECORDED"}, {"id": "FAM:SF-APPM-021", "score": 0.094, "document": "# FAM:SF-APPM-021 | Study family SF-APPM-021: Within-game tactical adaptation (halftime, crunch, post-run)\n- observed_research_question: Within-game tactical adaptation (halftime, crunch, post-run)\n- auto_outcome_status: not a hypothesis test\n- observed_hypothesis: Describe how play-type mix shifts by halftime margin, crunch time and after runs.\n- observed_result: OTHER +2.26 to +2.48pp in H2 in every bucket; crunch OTHER +6.08pp, PULL_UP +1.20pp at -0.045 PPP; post-run DRIVE +3.75pp, OTHER -6.72pp.\n- auto_failure_mechanism: E_unclear (mechanism unclear from the records)\n- auto_limited_by: LIM:label_or_classification_error\n- auto_limited_by: LIM:low_measurement_reliability\n- auto_has_risk: RISK:LABEL_ERROR\n- auto_has_risk: RISK:MEASURE_RELIABILITY\n- observed_failure_evidence: No inferential tests; OTHER label known to be noisy (PT audit).\n- observed_sample_and_power: NOT_RECORDED\n- auto_validation_status: no_validation_recorded; evidence: NOT_RECORDED\n- observed_verdict: descriptive\n- observed_failure_type: E_unclear\n- observed_notes: NOT_RECORDED"}, {"id": "FAM:SF-APPA-008", "score": 0.082, "document": "# FAM:SF-APPA-008 | Study family SF-APPA-008: Audit of overnight LLM adaptation-analysis batch ('9/10 adaptation predicts winning')\n- observed_research_question: Audit of overnight LLM adaptation-analysis batch ('9/10 adaptation predicts winning')\n- auto_outcome_status: artifact\n- observed_hypothesis: The agent batch's findings (defensive adjustments, point costs, adaptation predicts series winners 9/10) are valid.\n- observed_result: 706 findings with n<5; list_h2h_games called 0 times; series winners LLM-inferred; MIA-BOS G7 score invented (BOS 100 vs graph 84); what_came_next skips a possession; point costs are LLM arithmetic.\n- auto_failure_mechanism: C_artifact (apparent effect produced by a construction, selection or labelling flaw)\n- auto_limited_by: LIM:small_sample\n- auto_has_risk: RISK:SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER\n- observed_failure_evidence: Series winners never looked up (0/2073 calls); master '9/10' rule built from LLM inference on inference; low-n claims (e.g. 0.0 PPP on n=3).\n- observed_sample_and_power: NOT_RECORDED\n- auto_related_family: FAM:SF-APPA-009"}], "n_results": 4}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{"action": "final", "output": {
  "research_concepts": [
    "defensive effect on opponents' second-half minus first-half scoring",
    "team-level consistency of halftime adjustment",
    "stability and reliability of a team's ability to suppress opponent scoring change"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to isolate the defense's contribution to opponent H2-H1 scoring change from game script, opponent quality, and fatigue",
    "What reliability metric (split-half correlation, intra-class correlation, year-over-year consistency) is appropriate for a game-level derived team trait",
    "How to adjust for confounding factors such as pace, score margin, rest days, and home-court advantage"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-085",
      "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
      "finding": "Connector quality Q_p split-half reliability showed Pearson r=0.215, Spearman-Brown 0.355, ICC 0.216; the metric is too unreliable to be a stable player-season attribute, with most variance within-player half-to-half."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-005",
      "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
      "finding": "Defensive scheme change within and across games inferred from play-by-play labels showed near-uniform transition matrices, consistent with label noise; the inferred labels could not separate real defensive adjustment from offense execution changes."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPM-021",
      "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
      "finding": "Within-game tactical adaptation described play-type mix shifts by halftime margin and crunch time, but no inferential tests; OTHER play type known to be noisy."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-008",
      "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
      "finding": "An LLM-based analysis claiming defensive adjustments predict series winners was an artifact due to small samples, fabrications, and LLM-inferred labels without real data lookups."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-085",
      "what_failed": "Split-half reliability test of a per-game derived player-season attribute",
      "why": "Low measurement reliability: SPEARMAN-BROWN < 0.36, most variance within-player half-to-half."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-005",
      "what_failed": "Labeling defensive scheme change from play-by-play to test within-game adjustment",
      "why": "Label or classification error: inferred labels cannot distinguish real defensive adjustment from offensive execution, resulting in near-uniform transitions."
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "No retrieved record directly tested the reliability of a team's effect on opponent H2-H1 point change.",
    "FAM:SF-APPS-085 demonstrates that game-level derived metrics can have very low split-half reliability, making it hard to treat them as stable traits.",
    "FAM:SF-APPA-005 warns that inferring defensive behavior from play-by-play labels is prone to noise and may not capture true adjustment."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether a team's defensive effect on opponent H2-H1 change is a reliable team trait.",
    "The magnitude of between-team variance relative to within-team game variance for H2-H1 opponent scoring.",
    "Whether year-over-year or multi-season aggregation would yield adequate reliability."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The central challenge is that per-game H2-H1 opponent scoring differences are noisy; the bulk of variance likely comes from opponent quality, game script, and random shot-making rather than a stable defensive trait.",
    "A mixed-effects model with covariates could isolate a team-specific halftime adjustment effect, but the split-half reliability of that effect is expected to be low based on analogous failures (FAM:SF-APPS-085).",
    "The risk of concluding spurious reliability due to confounding or regression-to-the-mean artifacts is high if not carefully controlled."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "No historical estimate of the intraclass correlation of opponent H2-H1 change exists.",
    "The precise impact of controlling for opponent strength and game situation on the reliability estimate is unknown without simulation.",
    "Whether a large multi-season sample (120 team-seasons) could overcome within-game noise is an open question."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "yes",
    "explanation": "The retrieval did not directly address the question, but FAM:SF-APPS-085 shows a similar per-game derived trait failing split-half reliability, and FAM:SF-APPA-005 warns about inferring defensive adjustments from play-by-play labels. This evidence significantly increases our prior that the proposed trait will also be unreliable, shifting the decision toward do_not_proceed."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.3,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "Per-game opponent H2-H1 point differential is dominated by single-game noise; the signal may be too weak to yield a reliable team-level measure even with 82 games per season."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Game script (lead/deficit), opponent offensive strength, injuries, and rest days can contaminate the defensive effect estimate."
      },
      {
        "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
        "text": "Selecting teams based on extreme H2-H1 changes can create false consistency from regression to the mean."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "no",
    "cited": ["FAM:SF-APPS-085", "FAM:SF-APPA-005", "FAM:SF-APPM-021", "FAM:SF-APPA-008"],
    "summary": "No prior work examined the reliability of a team's effect on opponent H2-H1 scoring. The closest study (FAM:SF-APPS-085) evaluated split-half reliability of a similar per-game derived player attribute and found it untestable due to low measurement reliability. A study on inferred defensive scheme changes (FAM:SF-APPA-005) was untestable because of label noise."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "Game-level H2-H1 opponent point change has high intrinsic variance; the team signal may be swamped by noise, yielding low split-half or test-retest reliability."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Opponent quality, game location, rest, score effects, and pacing strategies can all affect H2-H1 change independently of any stable team defensive skill."
    },
    {
      "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
      "text": "Teams with unusually large or small H2-H1 changes in a subset of games will regress toward the mean, which can bias reliability estimates downward or upward if not accounted for."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "Testing reliability across multiple seasons, splits, or definitions without adjustment may inflate the false positive rate."
    },
    {
      "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
      "text": "If the study attempts to attribute the H2-H1 change to a particular defensive scheme or adjustment, inferred labels from play-by-play are known to be noisy (FAM:SF-APPA-005)."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.1,
    "rejected": 0.15,
    "untestable": 0.45,
    "artifact": 0.1,
    "uncertain": 0.2
  },
  "decision": "do_not_proceed",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The expected team-season causal effect on the opponent's points scored in the second half minus points scored in the first half, conditional on the opponent's offensive strength, game location, rest days, and the pre-game score margin expectation (to be proxied by opening point spread or first-half net rating).",
    "unit": "Team-season (120 team-seasons from 2022-23 to 2025-26).",
    "sample": "All regular-season games where both teams play a standard-length first and second half (excluding overtime to avoid extra period contamination). Exclude games with missing play-by-play or opponent data. Approximately 82 games per team-season, yielding about 9,840 team-game observations.",
    "power": "Simulation based on a mixed model: assuming a true between-team SD of 1.0 points in H2-H1 change and a within-game SD of 15 points (typical per-half scoring variance), with 82 games per team the standard error of the team mean is ~1.7. A split-half reliability analysis with ~41 games per half would have power to detect a Spearman-Brown reliability >0.7 if the true reliability is 0.8, but very low power if true reliability is below 0.4. Based on FAM:SF-APPS-085, plausible reliability is <0.4, so the study is underpowered to confirm even moderate reliability.",
    "controls": "Adjust via linear mixed model: fixed effects for opponent offensive rating (entering the game), game location (home/away), rest days (b2b indicator), and the first-half score margin (to separate game script from halftime adjustment). Random intercepts for team-season (the target trait) and opponent team-season.",
    "falsification": "1. Permute team-season labels within halves and recompute split-half correlation; true correlation should not exceed chance. 2. Test whether the team-season random intercept predicts H2-H1 change in held-out future games or the following season. 3. Check that the effect disappears when using only games where the halftime margin is extreme (as those games induce strategic changes unrelated to defensive skill).",
    "multiplicity": "Bonferonni correction over the number of halves (2) and seasons (4) if testing each separately; primary inference uses all data combined with a single split.",
    "decision_rule": "If the Spearman-Brown reliability of the estimated team-season BLUPs exceeds 0.60 and the difference from a null model (no team random effect) is significant at p < 0.01, conclude the trait is reliable. Otherwise, conclude insufficient evidence or unreliability.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 to 2025-26 per-game data from NBA stats API."
  }
}}

